WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.820
- Even guys in like
you know close quarters

00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:02.910
aren't gonna be like, yo
man, what's your sperm count?

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:03.743
- Yeah (laughs)

00:00:03.743 --> 00:00:05.260
- Nobody's, nobody's saying that.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:06.093
- No one.

00:00:06.161 --> 00:00:08.578
(soft music)

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.460
- Hi, my name is Phil, and I
have male factor infertility.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.460
So back in 2012, my wife Alex and I just

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:18.550
started this journey on okay,
we're gonna start a family.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:21.493
And we thought, oh gosh,
this is just gonna be easy,

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:23.940
and are we just prepared
for a baby in nine months?

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.933
And the reality was,

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.723
that journey that I thought was typical,

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.760
was for us, everything but typical.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.730
It was me, later that year getting checked

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.823
and realizing I had
male factor infertility.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.533
And so my sperm
development wasn't working.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.960
It wasn't happening the
way it should happen

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.703
and biologically for
whatever reason or another,

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:50.840
I had slow swimmers, I had weak swimmers,

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.170
I had swimmers that didn't
know where they were going,

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.563
and for me what it did
was, it started to lower,

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:00.720
day, by day, by day my own
intrinsic value of myself.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.260
I'm ready to talk to these guys

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.630
about what their scenario is

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.970
and see what we can find out about them

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.320
and what their journey might be.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.160
- Hey man, I'm Zach.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.020
- What's up man? I am Brendan.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:09.913
- I am Tristan.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.122
- What's up Tristan?
- What's going on?

00:01:11.122 --> 00:01:11.955
- How are you?

00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:13.523
- I'm good, kinda nervous.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:14.600
- Okay, don't be nervous.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:15.433
- Kinda nervous.

00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:19.160
- So like what's like a common
like sperm count for men?

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:22.603
And does it differ depending
on age group and age range?

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.283
- Yeah so actually, male
infertility in general,

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.250
is actually on the decrease.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.100
In fact, there's a handful
of studies right now

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.890
that have been produced
in the past six months

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.163
that are actually saying that years,

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.543
so let's say 30, 40, 50 years ago,

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:40.663
like a normal sperm count for
a man would be 100 million.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:41.793
- Okay.

00:01:41.793 --> 00:01:45.453
- Right now, we're sitting, at
least in the western culture,

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.203
somewhere around 50 million.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:49.373
So it's half,
- Right.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:50.990
- in the last 30, 40, 50 years.

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.670
One of the biggest
studies, was a Hebrew study

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.420
that was done at the University
of Jerusalem I believe,

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:58.483
and essentially what they said was,

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:02.783
we're 25 years behind in this
research of male infertility.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:05.310
They should have started back in the 70s

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.233
- [Zach] Right.

00:02:06.233 --> 00:02:08.563
- When they started to see
the decline in these numbers,

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:09.610
and then see the decline

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:11.510
in actual sperm health and development

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.200
and they didn't do anything about it

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.490
and now they're going,
this is kind of a crisis.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:17.820
- This seems like kind
of a dumb obvious one,

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.660
is that a lot of guys like

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:21.190
take for granted that
this won't be a problem.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:24.973
I guess how do I even find
out that I would be infertile?

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.270
- Brendan I would say
the first thing would be

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:31.630
if you and your spouse like
have been trying to have kids

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.250
and it's been over 12 months,

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:37.493
you know, they say that over
12 months of consistent trying,

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.663
month after month to have
children and it's not happening,

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.843
that's considered infertility.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.020
That's where we step in and we go okay,

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:49.140
I gotta be manly about this,
and I gotta step my game up

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.310
and I gotta think about

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.403
possibly is this a male factor thing?

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.670
Could I be a contributing
factor to our infertility?

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.580
And then you go in to a
clinic and you get tested,

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:00.953
and get a sperm test done,

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.800
and from there, you
know, try to figure out

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.950
if there's a diagnosis
or there's a reason.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:09.740
Sometimes infertility is
actually undiagnosed infertility

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.363
and there's not a specific reason for it.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:16.253
You could have great,
fast, strong swimmers,

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.200
and a ton of 'em,

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:20.010
and she could have no signs of let's say,

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:24.323
endometriosis, or PCOS, and
you just can't get pregnant.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.993
And at that point, it's
just trial and error.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.450
- I've had, you know, had unprotected sex

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:31.990
but I've never gotten anyone pregnant,

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:33.590
so I was always like, oh you know,

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:35.673
just good, just in the clear.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.090
But I've never thought like

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:39.690
oh I could just not be able
to get someone pregnant.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:40.523
- Sure.

00:03:40.523 --> 00:03:41.910
- So like when you found out,

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.793
how did that affect you mentally?

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:47.250
- [Phil] I think for a while
it was, this like gut punch,

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.150
- [Tristan] Right.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:49.833
- to my manhood.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:51.073
- Yeah.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.730
You know it was like, well
you're supposed to be macho,

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:56.943
and just strong,

00:03:56.976 --> 00:03:58.070
- [Tristan] Right.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:00.162
- and I wasn't that, like I felt weak.

00:04:00.162 --> 00:04:00.995
- Right.

00:04:00.995 --> 00:04:02.500
- Like for some reason I thought like,

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:07.120
my sperm count made my identity less.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.019
- You know we're talking about
like healthy, eating healthy.

00:04:09.019 --> 00:04:09.852
- Yup.

00:04:09.852 --> 00:04:11.520
- I've, last year, I lost like 50 pounds.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:12.353
- [Phil] Impressive.

00:04:12.353 --> 00:04:13.610
- [Zach] Yeah man, yeah
I did a really good job.

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:14.540
- [Phil] Do you feel better?

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:17.420
- I feel way better,
I feel like healthier.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:19.173
While we talk about like you know,

00:04:19.458 --> 00:04:21.040
like eating healthy and all that stuff,

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.660
does genetics, is it genetic?

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:23.983
Does it get passed down?

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.800
'Cause again, we don't know,
is that like something that,

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.750
- Yeah.
- is like an indicator or not?

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:29.710
- Kinda going back to it,

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:32.240
I think there needs to
be a lot of attention

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:35.090
in the medical field, so that
we can figure this problem out

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:39.240
because I know that there
are factors that contribute

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:42.860
to having a healthier reproductive
system for a male, right?

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:45.960
It's getting the fat off of
your body which creates heat

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:48.750
and that heat is killing the whole system

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:49.650
that's creating sperm.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:50.483
- Yup.

00:04:50.483 --> 00:04:51.810
- And so the hotter your body runs,

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.660
the less sperm you're likely to have.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.780
So that's why you see in
overweight populations,

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:00.050
there's usually more infertility
problems and diagnosis's

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:01.580
for male and female,

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:02.413
- Right.

00:05:02.413 --> 00:05:03.840
- because they're carrying so much more.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.040
- Sounds like too and
this is, I don't know this

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:08.040
but like I would assume that like,

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.090
you know, if you're going
down the road of like

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:13.123
trying the different like
treatments and the IVF and stuff,

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:15.110
that would probably get
relatively expensive.

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:15.943
- Yup.

00:05:15.943 --> 00:05:17.220
- And so I guess I'm curious like,

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:18.210
in the country right now,

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:20.730
is that something that's
frequently covered much

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:23.810
by insurance or is it like
really kinda hit or miss?

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:24.963
- And I would say,

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.653
for the majority of
infertility treatments,

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:32.053
most insurances don't cover.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.393
You're gonna find with big corporations,

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:41.330
that they do have some IVF extensions,

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.360
or there's like a bucket
within the company

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:44.330
that they'll be able to say

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:48.080
hey, we can help you with
x amount of IVF treatments

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:52.410
but as insurances alone,
it's really difficult.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.400
- Procreating and being able to have kids

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.193
is such a big thing.
- Sure.

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:58.540
- Do you ever feel like you
not being able to do it,

00:05:58.540 --> 00:05:59.373
she will leave?

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:01.400
- [Phil] You know what,
I did think of that.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.240
- That's like when I think
about being infertile,

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:04.990
that's my biggest fear,
- Yup.

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:07.580
- is like someone being like
well you can't give me a child,

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:08.750
why would I still be here?

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:10.260
- There were moments and there were days

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:12.110
that I would look at Alex and I'd go,

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:14.820
if I can't give this dream to you,

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:15.653
- Right.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:17.580
- like, here's your way out.
- Right.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:19.093
- And she never took it.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:22.443
And I'm so grateful that she didn't.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:23.943
- Good.

00:06:23.943 --> 00:06:28.358
- It's amazing because we're
not in this marriage for sex.

00:06:28.358 --> 00:06:29.191
- Right.

00:06:29.191 --> 00:06:31.300
- We're not in this marriage for babies,

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:34.583
